Circular Queue 
A Circular Queue is an advanced version of a linear queue where the last position is connected back to 
the first position, forming a circle. This allows the queue to efficiently utilize memory by reusing the 
spaces freed after elements are dequeued. It follows the FIFO (First In First Out) principle, meaning the 
element inserted first is removed first. 
Why Circular Queue? 
In a simple array implementation, we either make dequeue costly by shifting all elements, or use

t In First Out) principle, meaning the 
element inserted first is removed first. 
Why Circular Queue? 
In a simple array implementation, we either make dequeue costly by shifting all elements, or use 
a conceptually infinite array, which can lead to wasted memory. To overcome these problems, we use 
a circular queue, where the rear wraps around to the start, allowing both enqueue and dequeue to 
be O(1) and fully utilizing the array. 


